	(footprint "lib:LitzPad_15A"
		(layer "F.Cu")
		(uuid "4a18789b-b539-4a3f-aa9b-e6774788761d")
		(at 52.5 6 180)
		(descr "High-current litz wire termination pad, 15A rated. 8.0 mm dia pad, 3.0 mm dia hole. Dimension sourcing: generic high-current PTH pad spec; low confidence — no part-specific datasheet exists for this custom coil terminal. Flagged for human visual cross-check before fabrication. PITCH CORRECTED 2026-07-29 (docs/brainstorms/2026-07-29-hv-footprint-copper-shorts-requirements.md Case 2): this is R30, Sheetpath tank.inductor_conn -- the two ends of the resonant tank coil itself (pad 1 net tank.c_tank1-p2, pad 2 net tank-out; elec/src/modules.ato). The prior 5.0mm pitch on 8.0mm pads gave -3.0mm (3.0mm of copper OVERLAP), a fabrication-blocking short between the tank's two highest-differential-voltage nodes, unconditionally wrong regardless of the voltage question below. That 2026-07-29 revision set the pitch to 13.0mm = 8.0mm pad dia + 5.0mm creepage (SUPERSEDED 2026-08-12, see below; retained here because the reasoning that replaced it is only legible against it), where 5.0mm is docs/specs/HIGH_VOLTAGE_CLEARANCE_SPEC.md Sec 5.1's IEC 60335-1 Table 16 (Pollution Degree 2, Material Group IIIb) BASIC-insulation creepage figure at 400V -- the applicable category since both pads are the same tank circuit (not a mains/SELV boundary; c.f. scripts/generate_kicad_dru.py's separately-lower 'HV internal same footprint' 1.5mm, which is a TO-247-pin-pitch compromise for a manufacturer-fixed package and does not apply here since this footprint's geometry is fully our own choice). 400V is elec/src/modules.ato's declared v_tank_peak (asserted design floor, used elsewhere for c_tank1/c_tank2 voltage-rating margin). CAVEAT (1) OF THAT NOTE IS NOW CLOSED, AND IT RESOLVED AGAINST THE 13.0mm PITCH -- PITCH RE-DERIVED 2026-08-12 TO 18.0mm. The 2026-07-29 note said: 'if the finalized L pushes working voltage above 400V, this 13.0mm/5.0mm figure is a floor, not sufficient, and must be re-derived at the higher voltage ... a >400V case needs a fresh standard lookup, not extrapolation from this table'. That condition is met: the tank node's working voltage is now MEASURED, not assumed, at 570.5 Vrms / 923.7 V peak (docs/evidence/2026-08-12-hv-clearance-adequacy.md; this pad pair specifically carries tank.c_tank1-p2 <-> tank-out, i.e. essentially the tank<->PWR_RTN voltage at 544.6 Vrms -- same Table 18 row). The fresh lookup was done and it is a DIFFERENT table and a different pollution degree from the superseded one: IEC 60335-1 Table 18 (functional insulation creepage), band >500-800V, material group IIIa/IIIb, POLLUTION DEGREE 3 -> 10.0mm. PD3 governs, not the PD2 this footprint's previous derivation used: IEC 60335-2-6 cl. 29.2's Addition makes PD3 the default for cooking appliances, and the sealed compartment PD2 would be conditional on is both unbuilt and thermally counterproductive (docs/evidence/2026-08-12-pollution-degree-resolution.md; docs/evidence/2026-08-11-pd2-decision-record.md; docs/evidence/2026-08-12-hv-hv-creepage-determination.md for the Table 18 derivation). So the PD3/Table 18 lookup SUPERSEDES the PD2/Table 16 400V lookup that produced 13.0mm -- that older figure is not a floor that happened to be raised, it was read from a table that does not apply to this node. New pitch 18.0mm = 8.0mm pad dia + 10.0mm creepage; measured pad-edge-to-pad-edge gap is exactly 10.0mm. Coating this pair down to PD1 was considered and is NOT available: IEC 60664-3 cl. 4.3 requires the conductive part to be COVERED by the coating, and these two pads are bare litz-wire terminations that must stay uncoated to be soldered to. (2) STILL OPEN, unchanged and NOT closed by this revision, flagged for human sign-off before fabrication: the 8.0mm pad diameter itself remains the original 'generic ... low confidence, no part-specific datasheet' figure -- widening only the pitch does not source the diameter; confirm the actual lug/terminal size against a real 15A litz-wire termination spec before fabrication. Note that the diameter and the pitch are coupled: the 18.0mm pitch is derived AS 'diameter + 10.0mm', so if the diameter is later sourced at anything other than 8.0mm the pitch must move with it, or the 10.0mm creepage is silently lost. PAD DIAMETER SOURCED (partially -- see the still-open item below) AND PITCH RE-DERIVED 2026-08-13, CLOSING CAVEAT (2) ABOVE'S CURRENT-CAPACITY HALF: this pad is R30, the tank coil's own bare litz-wire solder termination, NOT a purchased connector -- confirmed against docs/hardware/TANK_COIL_SPECIFICATION.md req #10 ('two leads, tinned, for through-hole pads') and docs/hardware/BOM.md L_TANK line ('2 leads to LitzPad_15A pads'); docs/CONNECTORS_AND_WIRING.md's J_COIL M4-screw-terminal entry is a stale v1.0 (2025-12-17) document with no corresponding component in elec/src or docs/hardware/BOM.md and does not describe the as-built design. The name 'LitzPad_15A' is kept unchanged on purpose, the same way R30 keeps a deliberately wrong 'R' prefix (see modules.ato) -- renaming the footprint would desync it from the fp-lib-table reference baked into every R30 footprint instance already placed on pcb/temper.kicad_pcb (out of scope for this change), so the 15A in the name is now historical, not descriptive; the true rating is elec/src/footprints.ato's pad.current_rating field, updated alongside this file. The name was found to UNDER-declare, not over-declare: the tank carries 20.7A rms (this repo's ngspice harness) to 22.5A rms (independent first-harmonic solve, docs/hardware/TANK_COIL_SPECIFICATION.md Sec 3) continuous at the 1800W point -- compared like-for-like against this pad's declared 15A CONTINUOUS rating (not peak; a 28.7-31.9A peak-vs-15A-continuous comparison, as docs/hardware/PART_STRESS_AUDIT.md Sec 1.3 makes, mixes bases), the pad was already carrying 1.38-1.5x its declared continuous rating before this fix. Sized to `inductor_conn.current_rating = 25A rms` (modules.ato:620), the design's own imposed continuous-thermal figure for this exact part (TANK_COIL_SPECIFICATION.md req #6), not to the lower measured operating figures, so the fix carries the same ~1.11x margin over measured current that the rest of the design already relies on. IPC-2221B (docs/hardware/TRACE_WIDTH_CALCULATIONS.md Sec 2, I = k x dT^0.44 x A^0.725, external k=0.048) at this board's own 2oz/70um outer copper (TRACE_WIDTH_CALCULATIONS.md Sec 1) and this net's own established 40C rise allowance (TRACE_WIDTH_CALCULATIONS.md Sec 3.1/3.3/Sec4, 'HighVoltage'/'Resonant Tank' rows) requires A = (25 / (0.048 x 40^0.44))^(1/0.725) = 595.5 mils^2 = 0.384mm^2 of copper cross-section, i.e. an equivalent single-direction conductor width of 595.5 / (2 x 1.37) = 217.3 mils = 5.52mm. Applied here as the MINIMUM radial annular-ring width (hole wall to pad edge) -- an explicitly conservative translation of a straight-trace formula onto a round pad (it ignores the extra conducting cross-section a round pad's full 360 degree spread provides over a linear trace of the same width, so it is a floor, not a fit) -- rounded up to 6.0mm for fabrication margin (~9% above the computed 5.52mm minimum). Drill held at 3.0mm (litz-bundle physical fit; not re-examined by this pass, and STILL the unsourced figure from the original note -- see below). New pad diameter = 3.0mm drill + 2 x 6.0mm ring = 15.0mm. New pitch = 15.0mm diameter + 10.0mm PD3/Table 18 creepage (unchanged derivation from the 2026-08-12 revision above) = 25.0mm; measured pad-edge-to-pad-edge gap remains exactly 10.0mm. (2) IS THEREFORE ONLY PARTIALLY CLOSED: the pad diameter is now a computed, cited figure rather than a bare 'generic' guess, but it is still not a part-specific datasheet number -- it is this repo's own IPC-2221B trace-width method applied to a PTH annulus by engineering judgment, not a manufacturer's rated lug or terminal. Flagged for human visual cross-check before fabrication, same as before. The diameter/pitch coupling is unchanged and still binding: if 15.0mm is ever revised, the pitch must move with it (pitch = diameter + 10.0mm) or the creepage requirement is silently lost. See docs/evidence/2026-08-13-coil-connector-rating.md.")
		(tags "litz custom coil terminal high_current")
